Kaliyuga: The Bleeding Crown
The Full Story (v7) and Game Design. Part One (sections 1 to 24) is the story, cast, and full scripts for every act and both endings. Part Two (sections 25 to 40) is the game design: pitch, combat, progression, systems, art, audio, tech, scope, and risks. Anything I added is tagged [ADDED], so you can cut it.
1. Your decisions (locked)
The background is the war between gods and asuras, starting from Samudra Manthan.
The gods are losing, so they pour all their power into an unborn child, Rudra. His mother dies at the birth.
His father, Kaala-Chakra, was a good sage. The asuras corrupt him and he joins their side, and Rudra fights beside him.
Kaala-Chakra tells Rudra the gods killed his brother. This is a lie.
Rudra attacks heaven in revenge and kills a huge number of people.
Vishnu tears the Mani out of him, breaks it into seven shards, and throws it away. Rudra falls, and three thousand years later the game begins.
Every boss is an asura or rakshasa with a power from Hindu myth, and every boss matters to the story.
Added by me:
The victims were a camp of healers and families (the Shishira), not just soldiers.
Rudra’s mother’s soul is inside the Mani, and Kaala-Chakra’s real goal is to bring his wife back.
Savitri the ferrywoman and her daughter Ila.
Vishnu’s one line, “Go and look.”
The Look button, Sanjaya’s on-screen corrections, and two endings.
2. The world and the war
The Churning. Gods and asuras churn the ocean of milk together to win Amrita. Mount Mandara is the rod and the serpent Vasuki the rope. Halahala, a poison that would burn the world, rises first, and Shiva drinks it. Then the Amrita rises. Vishnu, as the enchantress Mohini, tricks the asuras out of their share. One asura, Rahu, slips into the gods’ line and drinks. Vishnu cuts off his head, but the nectar is already in his throat. The head lives on and swallows the sun, which is why eclipses happen.
The war. The asuras never forgive the cheat. Their guru Shukracharya holds the Sanjivani, a rite that raises the dead, so every asura killed by day stands up again at night. The gods start to lose.
The child. The gods’ last plan is one child who can hold all their power in a gem in his brow, the Mani. A mortal body can’t hold that much power alone, so the Mani needs a second soul to anchor it, from a mother who gives hers willingly. A sage’s wife, Suvarna, agrees. She does not tell her husband the price, and she dies in the birth. [ADDED: her soul stays inside the Mani.]
Both sides are guilty. The gods cheated first, and the asuras answered with centuries of killing. Rudra’s crime is not that he picked a side. It’s that he never asked a question.
3. The cast
Rudra. Raised as a weapon and taught never to question. Loyal, proud, and honest, but his loyalty has no brakes. His flaw is that he never checks. His arc is learning to look, and then to stay and answer for what he did.
Kaala-Chakra. Rudra’s father. A gentle, wise sage until Suvarna died. He blamed the gods. Shukracharya offered the Sanjivani to bring her back, if he joined the asuras and gave them his son’s power. Each compromise made the next one easier. He never loved Rudra. He loved Suvarna, and Rudra is the vessel that holds her. [ADDED]
Nachiketa. Rudra’s older brother, a healer who bandaged birds as a boy and soldiers of both sides as a man. He’s the one who could have stopped it, and he appears only as absence: a letter, a sparrow, a tent.
Savitri. The last ferrywoman of the Vaitarani, the river of the dead, and the only living mortal in the story. Her daughter Ila, sixteen, died in the camp. She knows exactly who Rudra is and pulls him out anyway, because only he can climb the mountain where Ila’s soul is held. Her lantern shows what is real and burns her remaining years. She is also using him, and she knows it. [ADDED]
Ila. Savitri’s daughter. She stole sweets for her patients, treated asura wounded in the same tent as gods (“a wound has no side”), and kept a sparrow with a broken wing. She never appears. [ADDED]
Vishnu. The god who arrives too late. He says “Go and look,” breaks the Mani to free the souls trapped in it, and never kills Rudra. Afterward he waits outside a locked gate, because opening it would destroy the souls that hold it shut. For public release, call him the Preserver. [ADDED]
Sanjaya. A blind narrator and the Council’s court chronicler. The story he tells is the one he was given, and the game crosses out his words as the truth arrives. [ADDED]
The Hound of Doomsday. Rudra’s own death, which has waited three thousand years. It’s an interlude, not a boss fight.
4. What really happened
Kaala-Chakra performs the rite that puts the gods’ power into his unborn son. Suvarna’s soul is the anchor, and he learns the price only after she dies. [ADDED]
Shukracharya offers him the Sanjivani, which needs a huge amount of life-force. The war can supply it, so he joins the asuras.
He plans one great harvest. Rudra will fire the Brahmashira, a weapon that burns souls loose, at a crowd. The Mani will catch every soul, and the Sanjivani will then melt it into resurrection light and bring Suvarna back. Pulling the Mani out of Rudra’s bone will kill him. [ADDED]
Nachiketa, working in the healers’ camp, finds the ledger. He copies one page and writes to Ila: “I will tell Rudra at dawn.” [ADDED]
On the Eighteenth Night a truce is signed. Kaala-Chakra brings Rudra a shrouded body and says the gods killed Nachiketa. It is a dead soldier. He has also moved families into a citadel on a hill “for protection.” He points and says: “Killers.” Rudra sees lamps in the windows. “Watchfires.” Two hundred paces away a child laughs. Rudra does not walk down. He fires.
Forty thousand die, among them Nachiketa, in a tent his father chose. [ADDED]
Vishnu arrives too late. Kaala-Chakra steps between them, says “Run,” and falls. Vishnu never touches him, because Time was held for one breath so it looks that way. Rudra’s last memory is the god killing his father.
Vishnu says, “Rudra. Go and look.” Rudra doesn’t hear it and attacks. Vishnu tears the Mani out and breaks it into seven shards to free the souls. It fails, because the souls cling to the shards. Rudra can’t die while they hold him, so he falls into the river of the dead.
Kaala-Chakra keeps the seventh shard, the heart, in his third eye. He gives the other six to six asuras as guards, telling each: “Your dead are inside the stone.” Four believe it. The twin kings take theirs by force. He seals heaven with the camp’s souls as the lock, which locks Vishnu out. [ADDED]
He can’t rebuild the Mani himself, because it rejoins only on its bearer’s brow. So he waits three thousand years for his undying son to climb the mountain and bring him the shards. [ADDED]
5. How the story is told
Look. One button. Rudra stops and looks at a lamp, a toy, a tent flap, a ledger leaf. It’s optional and adds a thread. On the flashback hill, Look does nothing and the path is blocked. In the Vow ending, it’s open.
Sanjaya’s corrections. His narration is crossed out and rewritten on screen as shards return the truth. There are seven.
Shards are memories. Each returns one true scene from the Night of Ash.
The Ferry. Between acts, Rudra and Savitri cross the river. Two people and a lantern. These scenes are the heart of the game.
The lantern. When Rudra dies, he wakes in Savitri’s boat. Her voice gets quieter and the light cooler each time. It’s a story beat, never a punishing stat.
