33. Controls and UI
PC. WASD to move, mouse or keys to aim and attack, Space to dash, Q or Tab to switch stance, E to Look, F to Burn (Act V), Esc to pause. Fully remappable.
Gamepad. Left stick move, face buttons for light and heavy, bumper for stance, trigger for dash, a dedicated button for Look. Treat gamepad as a first-class input.
HUD. Health, stamina, posture bar (bosses), stance indicator, and the lantern in a corner, so the player always sees Savitri’s light. No quest markers.
Accessibility.
Subtitles on by default, with speaker names and strikethrough preserved.
Look can be toggle or hold.
A “Story” difficulty with more health and wider dash windows, so no one is blocked from the ending.
Color-safe posture and telegraph colors.
No flashing during the Brahmashira scene without a warning.
34. Art Direction
Prologue. Painted scroll panels with rough brushwork, dark gold and ash, as if illustrated for a temple wall. Sanjaya’s voice over the paint.
In-game. Stylized, painterly 3D with strong silhouettes. Rudra is a broad figure with a furnace in his brow that glows and dims with his state.
Palette by act. I: ash-grey and ochre. II: violet and teal. III: forge-orange and blue-black. IV: gold and green twilight. V: white and storm-purple. VI: black and gold.
Light rule. Savitri’s lantern is the only warm light in the game, apart from fire. When it dims the world goes cool.
Boss design. Each boss has one readable silhouette and one signature visual detail from the myth (Vritra’s river-belly, Raktabeeja’s blood-drops).
Respect. Draw the mythology the way it is told: with care, not as spectacle. Deities are never mocked.
35. Audio and Music
The lullaby. Suvarna’s tune. Solo female voice or sarangi. It appears in the Mani’s hum, in Ila’s humming, and in the final walk. It is never played in full until the end.
The drum. Dundubhi’s war-drum is the first thing the player hears and the last thing the Council used to call the families. Its rhythm recurs corrupted and slow in the Furnace.
The ferry. A tanpura drone, water, and silence. Almost no music, so the voices carry.
Look moments. No music. Only room tone and the object.
Boss themes. One instrument per boss from Indian tradition: conch, ghatam, rudra veena, mridangam.
Voice. Rudra, Savitri, Sanjaya, and Kaala-Chakra are fully voiced. Others can be partial. Dialogue must be recorded cleanly, because the ferry scenes depend on it.
