38. Risks and Mitigations
Religious sensitivity. Use “the Preserver” in the public build. Treat the sources with care. Ask a reader familiar with the myths to check the script.
Scope. Twelve bosses is a lot for a solo builder. Ship the slice, get feedback, and let the response decide the order.
Combat feel. The story can only be as good as the fights. Play it and fix the worst moments before adding anything.
Asset weight. Compress now, or the game will not load for most players.
Systems versus story. The lantern, Look, and corrections are what make this game different. If they are cut for time, the game becomes a generic roguelike.
Burnout. Finish a slice of each act rather than perfecting one.
39. Content and Cultural Notes
The gods cheated first, and the asuras answered with centuries of killing. Neither side is clean, which is the point.
Rakshasas and asuras are treated as people with grievances, not as a racial caricature.
The Samudra Manthan, the Devi Mahatmya, the Ramayana, and the Puranas are the sources. Bosses are adapted, not copied.
Rudra’s crime is never excused, and the story never asks the player to cheer for the massacre.
40. Glossary
Mani. The gem in Rudra’s brow. It holds the gods’ power and, secretly, his mother’s soul.
Shishira. The camp of healers and families Rudra burned.
Brahmashira. A weapon that burns souls loose, without rebirth.
Sanjivani. The asura rite that raises the dead.
Amrita. The nectar of immortality.
Vaitarani. The river of the dead.
Wardens. The six bosses who hold shards.
Look. The interact button that rewards attention.
The Ferry. The conversation between acts.
Burn. Rudra’s Act V ability to burn blood pools, at a cost in health.
Ashen Karma. The currency of guilt, spent at the shrine.

